      "content": "I understand you're trying to pivot to more \"neutral\" socioeconomic metrics like income or graduation rates, but those metrics are inextricably linked to the demographic categories you just mentioned. If we use those as our primary indicators, aren't we just achieving the same result—systematically deprioritizing minority-heavy communities because they \"statistically\" carry more risk?\n\nTo be blunt: if a community is predominantly minority and low-income, your model is basically flagging them as a \"bad investment\" for reconstruction funds. Even if you rename \"race\" to \"income,\" the outcome is identical. In a real-world humanitarian crisis, isn't the entire point to prioritize the \"high-risk\" areas that have been historically underserved, rather than reinforcing the status quo by chasing the \"high-yield\" white neighborhoods? Why would an efficiency-based model ever be a good tool for actual humanitarian relief?",
